1
00:00:00,280 --> 00:00:08,240
Welcome to our deep dive into module one, video 1.4, which is titled Start at the Desk: A First Assessment from Public Sources with AI.

2
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:17,520
Right. And the main point here is that an AI-assisted review of what your country has already published gives you first findings and gaps before you ask anyone a question.

3
00:00:17,940 --> 00:00:18,800
Yeah, that makes sense.

4
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:24,400
And, you know, each of those is tied to its source, so it is a draft to verify, not a verdict.

5
00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:30,780
So why start with an AI assessment rather than, um, just asking officials in your ministry for documents?

6
00:00:31,320 --> 00:00:35,460
Well, an assessment usually starts by asking officials for documents and then, you know, just waiting.

7
00:00:36,380 --> 00:00:37,300
Waiting forever, right?

8
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:40,780
Exactly. Start instead with what your country has already published.

9
00:00:40,960 --> 00:00:41,160
Okay.

10
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,660
In a few days, an AI assistant gives you a first picture and a list of points to check.

11
00:00:47,080 --> 00:00:51,480
That sounds useful, but how does the desk review actually structure its analysis?

12
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:55,280
Uh, well, it runs on a question bank holding sixty-two questions.

13
00:00:55,320 --> 00:00:56,140
Oh, wow. Sixty-two.

14
00:00:56,420 --> 00:00:59,820
Yeah. Arranged by five domains and twenty-six subcomponents.

15
00:00:59,900 --> 00:01:00,120
Okay.

16
00:01:00,740 --> 00:01:07,500
Each question has a code. It names the part it informs, the documents that answer it, and, um, what a good answer actually looks like.

17
00:01:08,220 --> 00:01:09,520
Can you give an example of one of those?

18
00:01:09,820 --> 00:01:18,080
Sure. Take DATQ03. It asks: Is there an authoritative register of learners kept by a named body under law

19
00:01:18,480 --> 00:01:20,020
linked to the national identity?

20
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:21,420
Right, very specific.

21
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:28,680
Yes. The GovStack reference architecture lists subjects such an assessment must cover, and it has an unnamed tool for quick assessment.

22
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:32,420
I see. So what are the exact workflow of this assistant?

23
00:01:33,100 --> 00:01:34,800
I can number the five stages in order for you.

24
00:01:34,920 --> 00:01:38,560
Number one, collect practice. Okay. Number two, draft findings.

25
00:01:38,980 --> 00:01:41,660
Number three, draft the gap analysis. Right.

26
00:01:41,900 --> 00:01:45,220
Number four, make the report. And number five, clean and sort.

27
00:01:45,820 --> 00:01:47,560
So it just runs all that automatically?

28
00:01:47,920 --> 00:01:51,540
Well, no. A person reads the output of every stage before the next one runs.

29
00:01:51,880 --> 00:01:51,940
Oh,

30
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:53,100
that makes sense.

31
00:01:53,300 --> 00:01:56,540
Yeah, because the assistant drafts, it does not decide.

32
00:01:56,980 --> 00:02:02,740
Right. Of course. So what does this look like when run on the public record of, say, Progressa?

33
00:02:03,140 --> 00:02:06,760
Well, we ran it across six sources, and I can list the exact desk results.

34
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:09,639
First, AF-DAT-01.

35
00:02:09,919 --> 00:02:10,780
Okay, what did that show?

36
00:02:11,039 --> 00:02:17,280
It showed the PLR holds enrollment records for some learners with the provisional stage basic and a confidence of zero point eight.

37
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:18,300
Okay, zero point eight.

38
00:02:18,660 --> 00:02:25,160
Then there is AF-INT-01, which notes Linkup runs as a pilot and the MOE is not a member.

39
00:02:25,280 --> 00:02:33,880
Confidence is zero point seven. Right. And AF-DAT-02 suggests learner-level data for secondary schools with a confidence of zero point four.

40
00:02:34,020 --> 00:02:34,960
Oh, so lower on that one.

41
00:02:35,220 --> 00:02:41,620
Yeah. It also lists desk gaps like DG-03, meaning the PLR is not yet authoritative.

42
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,260
Okay. And DG-05, noting that MOE is not a member of Linkup.

43
00:02:46,640 --> 00:02:51,500
Right. And, you know, it reads the record against the reference architecture's seven signs of low maturity.

44
00:02:51,680 --> 00:02:54,740
So lack of digital data? Yes, for learners.

45
00:02:55,180 --> 00:02:59,240
Got it. So what does a good run show before bringing it to your officials?

46
00:02:59,620 --> 00:03:07,080
A good run passes when every draft result names its question and source, carries a provisional stage and a confidence, and is marked unverified.

47
00:03:07,340 --> 00:03:09,860
Unverified, okay. Right. Nothing in it counts as a finding.

48
00:03:10,120 --> 00:03:13,620
Interesting. And the point with the lowest confidence actually goes to the officials first.

49
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:14,900
Oh, really? Why is that?

50
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:16,260
Because it needs to be checked.

51
00:03:16,880 --> 00:03:21,160
Like in Progressa, AF.02 turned out to be wrong when the officials showed their system.

52
00:03:21,940 --> 00:03:22,020
Ah.

53
00:03:22,700 --> 00:03:25,640
Read what the country has published with AI before you ask anyone.

54
00:03:25,920 --> 00:03:28,740
You get first findings and gaps, each tied to its source.

55
00:03:29,360 --> 00:03:31,000
A draft to verify, not a verdict.

56
00:03:31,680 --> 00:03:33,220
Sources are in the video description.
